      "message": "Issue: When bmcActiveFirmwareId is unknown (e.g., getActiveBmcFirmware failed — it swallows errors with console.log) or when the active BMC entry is not marked Updateable, the getter silently defaults to the first inventory member. That may be a BIOS image, a CPLD, or a backup BMC slot depending on the ordering returned by bmcweb.\n\nImpact: The commit message explicitly promises \"The dropdown defaults to the bmc_active entry to preserve existing behavior.\" In the fallback path, it does not — the user may unknowingly upload firmware to whichever inventory member Redfish happened to list first.\n\nRecommendation: Return null in the fallback so the dropdown forces an explicit selection (or keeps the store action\u0027s legacy Targets \u003d [bmcPath] behaviour). This also removes the \"surprise target\" risk without breaking systems that expose bmc_active.\n```\nwatch: {\n  updateTargetOptions(options) {\n    if (\n      this.userSelectedTarget \u0026\u0026\n      !options.some((o) \u003d\u003e o.value \u003d\u003d\u003d this.userSelectedTarget)\n    ) {\n      this.userSelectedTarget \u003d null;\n    }\n  },\n},\n```",
